ntdll: A wait recorder that can be switched on in a running process (Squark).

Which thread waits, how long, on what, and which thread ended the wait, for a few seconds of a running
program, without restarting it. Off unless the folder $WINEPREFIX/squark-waitlog (or $SQUARK_WAITLOG_DIR)
exists when a process starts; then a thread of unix/waitlog.c sleeps in kqueue on that folder, and a new
"ctl" file there (id=, exe=, seconds=, warmup=, events=, out=) starts one window in each process whose
image name matches. Events go to a buffer allocated before the window and are written out after it, by
that thread: <out>/waitlog-<id>-<exe>-<pid>.events (40-byte records) and .json (thread ids, Mach thread
ids and CPU times at both ends of the window, thread names, object types).

Recorded on macOS, where ntsync does not exist:
- server waits: NtWaitForSingleObject, NtWaitForMultipleObjects (win32u's message waits too),
  NtSignalAndWaitForSingleObject, keyed events, NtRemoveIoCompletion(Ex), alertable NtDelayExecution;
  NtSetEvent, NtPulseEvent, NtReleaseSemaphore, NtReleaseMutant with their round trip.
  The wineserver puts the waking thread's id and request code into the unused __pad of the
  wake_up_reply it writes to the waiting thread's pipe (0x80000000 alone: a timeout, timer or fd); a
  wait satisfied by the select request itself is marked as such. The protocol does not change: an older
  client ignores the field, an older server leaves it 0.
- NtWaitForAlertByThreadId (critical sections, SRW locks, condition variables, barriers, WaitOnAddress,
  all through RtlWaitOnAddress) with the thread that called NtAlertThreadByThreadId. RtlWaitOnAddress
  now passes the address to NtWaitForAlertByThreadId, as Windows does, with what ntdll waited for in
  the top byte on 64-bit (1 WaitOnAddress, 2 critical section, 3/4 SRW exclusive/shared, 5 condition
  variable, 6 barrier); nothing else reads it.
- non-alertable NtDelayExecution (Sleep), and NtYieldExecution / Sleep(0), whose runs closer than
  yieldgap_us (default 100) become one event.

While off, an instrumented call costs one load of waitlog_state and a not-taken branch; no system
call. Waits that began before a window started are not recorded; the window starts warmup seconds
(default 0.5) after recording does. Waits still in progress at its end are written as open.
Waits outside ntdll (a Vulkan driver's, Cocoa's) are not recorded: the CPU times show them as time
neither on the CPU nor in a recorded wait. squark's tools/waitlog/waitlog.py reads the files.

